      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "The Toronto Blue Jays found a semblance of identity in the most brutal way possible: by grinding out a 1-0 shutout victory over a struggling Chicago White Sox. <strong>Shane Bieber led the charge, tossing six scoreless innings with six strikeouts while three relievers combined to complete the four-hitter</strong>, snapping Chicago's four-game winning streak and giving the Jays their first win in five meetings against them. It's a small balm for a team sitting 46-52, fifth in the AL East, and 10.5 games behind the division leader after collapsing 4-12 the night before. Vladimir Guerrero Jr. reached base three times and scored the lone run on George Springer's fourth-inning single, but this win was built on pitching, not offense. The rotation is finally delivering, even if the offense remains anemic. <strong>Trey Yesavage takes the ball today at 12:15 PM ET in the series finale, looking to secure the series win and keep this fragile momentum alive</strong>.",
        "updated": "Updated July 19, 2026"
      },
